## 5. How it works

**Stack.** `top` holds the index of the newest item and is `-1` when the stack is empty. `push` checks for a full stack, increments `top` and stores the value. `pop` checks for an empty stack, copies out `data[top]` and decrements `top`. `peek` reads the top value without changing `top`.

**Circular queue.** `front` is the index of the oldest item and `count` is the number of stored items. The next free slot is `(front + count) % SIZE`, so the index wraps back to 0 at the end of the array. `dequeue` returns `data[front]`, moves `front` to `(front + 1) % SIZE` and decrements `count`.

Every function returns `0` on failure (full or empty) and `1` on success, so errors are checked instead of corrupting memory.

## 6. Big O analysis

| Operation | Time | Space | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| `stack_push` | O(1) | O(1) | One comparison, one increment, one store |
| `stack_pop` | O(1) | O(1) | One comparison, one read, one decrement |
| `stack_peek` | O(1) | O(1) | One comparison and one read |
| `stack_isEmpty` / `stack_isFull` | O(1) | O(1) | One comparison |
| `enqueue` | O(1) | O(1) | One modulo calculation and one store |
| `dequeue` | O(1) | O(1) | One read and one modulo update |
| `queue_isEmpty` / `queue_isFull` | O(1) | O(1) | One comparison |

Both structures use O(n) storage overall, where n = `SIZE`, the fixed array length.

## 7. Design decision

The queue is **circular** and tracks `front` and `count`. The alternative was a plain array where every `dequeue` shifts all remaining items one place left, which costs O(n) per dequeue. Wrapping with `% SIZE` keeps `dequeue` at O(1). Tracking `count` also removes the ambiguity between a full and an empty queue, which happens when only `front` and `rear` are compared.

## 8. Limitation

The size is fixed at compile time (`SIZE 5`). Adding to a full structure fails instead of growing. A dynamic array or a linked list would remove this limit, at the cost of extra memory management.

## 9. Tests covered

- `pop` on an empty stack returns failure.
- `push` on a full stack returns failure.
- Stack returns items in last-in, first-out order.
- Queue wrap-around: enqueue until full, dequeue two, enqueue two more, then dequeue and check order.

